export const bundledGuides:Record<string,string>={
  "prepare": "# Before the audit: prepare a clean record\n\nThe goal is to pay the right premium. Start early, reconcile the records and preserve the paperwork that supports each proposed exclusion. Never alter a record to fit a desired outcome.\n\n## 1. Get the full policy and the carrier’s instructions\n\nAsk the carrier or your agent for the declarations, classification schedules, rating worksheets, coverage forms and **all endorsements**. A certificate of insurance is not the policy. Get the audit request, submission method, policy dates and deadline in writing. Ask which forms and date conventions the auditor expects.\n\nRecord the policy number, insured legal entities, locations, effective date, expiration or cancellation date, deposit premium and any midterm changes. Keep a copy of every submission and its receipt. Use a secure delivery method agreed with the recipient.\n\n## 2. Collect the accounting package\n\n| Record | Where / who to ask | What to check | Potential effect |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Policy-period P&L | Accountant or bookkeeper | Fiscal-year bridge, entities, locations | Explains payroll and revenue totals |\n| Payroll register | Payroll provider | Employee, work dates, duties, class, gross pay, extra OT premium | Supports class splits and allowed deductions |\n| General ledger detail | Accountant | Payroll, sales, subs, reimbursements and exclusion accounts | Finds duplicate or misallocated exposure |\n| Four quarterly 941s | Payroll provider / accountant | Partial-quarter reconciliation to the policy | Explains differences from tax returns |\n| 940 | Accountant | Annual payroll bridge | Checks completeness |\n| Florida RT-6 reports | Payroll provider / Florida DOR account | Quarters intersecting policy dates | Corroborates state payroll |\n| W-2s / W-3 | Payroll provider | Calendar year versus policy year | Explains wage totals |\n| 1099s, sub ledger and contracts | Accountant / project manager | Entity, work description, dates and amounts | Separates subs from other vendors |\n| Sub COIs | Sub or issuing agent | Correct entity, coverage line and all dates worked | May support WC charge reversal; GL depends on policy |\n| Individual FL DWC exemptions | Officer and DWC | Eligibility, entity, industry and effective dates | May support eligible officer exclusion |\n\nTaxable wages are not automatically premium payroll. Reconcile rather than forcing the reports to match. Keep the original exports plus a separate worksheet explaining every adjustment.\n\n## 3. Build a WC exposure schedule\n\nFor each employee or supported group, list actual duties, class code from the policy, payroll for the exact period, extra overtime premium, any other proposed excluded pay and the supporting rule. Keep time records for class allocations; a job title or an unsupported percentage is not a payroll split.\n\nFor each officer, document the person’s role, period-specific applicable minimum and maximum, units (weekly / annual), weeks or other duration basis, and any eligible exemption. Do not apply one person’s cap once for every class. Put special situations such as leased employees, PEOs, maritime work or wrap-ups in the case notes and get specialist review.\n\nObtain the mod worksheet and each factor’s effective dates. If a factor changes during the policy, preserve actual exposure records for each segment; do not assume payroll is evenly distributed through the year.\n\n## 4. Build a separate GL schedule\n\nFor every GL class, confirm the policy’s rating basis and denominator: payroll, gross sales, subcontract cost or units. Document exclusions individually. Separate insured and uninsured subcontractor costs, but do not remove insured costs merely because you have a COI. A different rate or retained charge may apply. See [GL](gl.md).\n\n## 5. Resolve gaps and send a complete package\n\nUse the app’s status values: missing, requested, received, verified or not applicable. For each item record who was asked, when, entity, dates covered, document reference and follow-up. “Received” is not “verified.” Explain non-applicable items.\n\nUse the [carrier](templates/carrier-request.md), [accountant](templates/accountant-request.md) and [subcontractor](templates/subcontractor-request.md) drafts. Ask the auditor whether any records can follow later and confirm the deadline; do not assume an extension. Keep submitted totals and source documents together.\n\nSources and current-value checks: [Florida register](states/fl.md). F.S. 440.381 provides the Florida payroll audit context; the policy and applicable manuals determine the rating treatment.\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n",
  "review": "# After the audit: explain the bill before disputing it\n\nWork from the complete audit worksheet and final bill. An invoice showing only “additional premium” is not enough to reconstruct the audit. Ask for the exposure, rate, basis, class and every adjustment.\n\n## Reconcile the premium, line by line\n\n1. Match the insured, coverage line, policy dates and locations. Bridge accounting periods to those dates.\n2. For each WC class and effective period, calculate chargeable payroll divided by 100, multiplied by the policy rate. For GL, divide the chargeable class exposure by the **specified rate denominator**, then multiply by its rate.\n3. For WC, apply the verified experience mod to the proper exposure segment. Confirm the worksheet’s effective date, not just its displayed factor.\n4. Follow the policy’s actual order for credits, discounts, minimum premium, expense constant and assessments. Determine which elements are subject to the mod and which assessment base applies. There is no universal sequence in this tool.\n5. Compare final premium with the deposit and all prior premium payments or credits. A negative final-minus-deposit balance is a return premium; a positive amount is additional premium.\n\nIn the app, each exposure trace shows a line charge. Premium-element traces show the running subtotal. Full precision is retained through the calculation and the result is rounded to cents; verify the carrier’s rounding convention. An unsupported value withholds the final estimate.\n\n## Look for explainable differences\n\n| Question | Evidence / next step |\n| --- | --- |\n| Wrong class or work description? | Actual operations, duties, job records and the carrier’s class-rule explanation |\n| Payroll split ignored? | Contemporaneous employee / duty / class records; do not invent percentage allocations |\n| Extra OT premium included? | Payroll detail separating ordinary pay from only the additional premium portion |\n| Officer limit or exemption ignored? | Individual identity, applicable period values and dated DWC verification |\n| Sales or payroll outside the period? | Date bridge, transactions, payroll and cancellation terms |\n| Sub charged despite coverage? | Correct line of insurance, legal entity, all dates worked, issuer confirmation and policy authority |\n| GL basis or denominator wrong? | Class schedule and supporting ledger; WC rules do not settle GL treatment |\n| Rate or mod wrong? | Policy endorsements, rate pages, full mod worksheet and effective periods |\n| Deposit credit missing? | Payment ledger and prior bills or refunds |\n| Minimum or assessment different? | Filed/policy value, exact base and calculation sequence |\n\nThe software does not choose a legally correct class or decide coverage. Use its manual review flags to identify the question and the evidence needed. A flag is not proof of an error.\n\n## Recover records left out of the original submission\n\nAsk whether the carrier will accept late COIs, eligible individual exemption certificates, OT premium detail and supported payroll splits. Document original dates; do not backdate or alter records. Ask for re-audit rules and deadlines in writing.\n\nThe app estimates the standalone effect of entered OT, officer-limit, exemption, insured-sub and GL-exclusion adjustments by repricing a scenario before that adjustment. A minimum premium or another factor can reduce the effect to zero. These individual effects can overlap and **must not be added**. The overall carrier-versus-proposed difference is a separate reconciliation, not an extra saving. Missing rates or authority mean **TODO**, not zero savings.\n\nFor a proposed class or rate change, make a JSON backup, run a separate case with the supported changed input and compare the complete results. Preserve the original worksheet. State assumptions and identify the evidence that would support the change.\n\nNext: [Dispute](dispute.md). Source register: [Florida](states/fl.md).\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n",
  "dispute": "# Request a correction with evidence\n\nA focused request is easier to review than a general objection to the bill. Identify the line, what the carrier used, the proposed correction, its authority and the record that supports it. Include increases as well as decreases when the reconciliation requires them.\n\n## Build the packet\n\n- **Cover letter:** insured, policy, period, audit date, contact, disputed items and requested action.\n- **Findings:** one question per item; distinguish an established record fact from an interpretation to verify.\n- **Evidence index:** document name, issuer, entity, relevant dates, page and the finding it supports. Only call an item “attached” when it is attached.\n- **Proposed worksheet:** exposure, basis, denominator, rate, mod periods, premium sequence, final and deposit reconciliation. Mark unknowns TODO.\n- **Requests:** full original worksheet, authority for rejected adjustments, confirmation of receipt, re-audit procedure and deadlines, and a corrected bill if supported.\n\nDownload the app’s Markdown draft or use [the packet template](templates/dispute-packet.md). It does not submit, sign or send the packet. Have a licensed professional review it before use.\n\n## Route and deadline tracker\n\n| Route | Use | Deadline to record | Authority to obtain |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Written carrier dispute | Start here for WC and GL | **TODO: policy/carrier-specific** | Audit letter, policy and written carrier response |\n| Carrier re-audit | Additional records or a corrected exposure schedule | **TODO: confirm acceptance window and requirements** | Carrier re-audit instructions and applicable manual / law |\n| NCCI Dispute Resolution | Unresolved WC NCCI manual-rule matters, subject to state process | **TODO: confirm applicable filing limits and prerequisites** | Current Basic Manual state process / NCCI written confirmation |\n| Florida DFS Consumer Services | Premium concerns and assistance with insurer response | **TODO: confirm any applicable limit** | DFS and policy-specific requirements |\n| Florida OIR | Ask about the proper regulatory route | **TODO: confirm jurisdiction and timing** | OIR direction; obtain professional help for legal filing limits |\n\nDo not substitute a generic number of days for the actual deadline. Keep the earliest confirmed date and its source in the case. Confirm what action preserves rights and whether a written extension is available. Asking for records, filing a complaint or requesting a re-audit does not automatically suspend payment, collection or other deadlines.\n\n[NCCI’s overview](https://www.ncci.com/Articles/Pages/UW_disputeresolutionprocess.aspx) covers manual-rule disputes and prerequisites, including attempting resolution with the carrier and payment of undisputed premium. Confirm current state requirements directly; NCCI is not the GL route.\n\n[Florida DFS insurance help](https://www.myfloridacfo.com/division/consumers/needourhelp) and [OIR contact information](https://www.floir.gov/about-us/contact-us) identify assistance routes. Do not use workers’ compensation medical reimbursement dispute procedures for an employer’s premium audit.\n\n## After the response\n\nKeep the carrier’s acceptance or rejection for every item. Reconcile a corrected bill to the accepted worksheet and deposit ledger. If a matter remains unresolved, organize the correspondence chronologically and ask the appropriate route about next steps. Do not stop communicating simply because you believe the bill is wrong.\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n",
  "florida": "# Florida module · WC / NCCI\n\nReference check: November 1, 2026. Always verify current law and the edition effective for the policy being audited. The app’s state source interface is `/app/src/states/fl.ts`; GL remains in a separate engine module.\n\n| Source | What to verify |\n| --- | --- |\n| [F.S. Chapter 440 / 440.381](https://www.leg.state.fl.us/statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0400-0499/0440/Sections/0440.381.html) | Payroll audit framework and required records; review the actual current statute for obligations |\n| [Audit rule 69O-189.003](https://flrules.org/gateway/ruleNo.asp?id=69O-189.003) | Effective application / audit procedures and referenced manuals |\n| [NCCI Basic Manual / Atlas](https://www.ncci.com/Pages/Atlas.aspx) | Correct edition, class assignment, payroll allocation, premium elements, Florida exceptions and miscellaneous values; licensed access may be needed |\n| [DWC exemptions](https://www.myfloridacfo.com/division/wc/employer/exemptions) | Individual eligibility and effective dates; distinguish construction / nonconstruction |\n| [NCCI Dispute Resolution](https://www.ncci.com/Articles/Pages/UW_disputeresolutionprocess.aspx) | Applicability, prerequisites, state procedures and deadlines |\n| [DFS Consumer Services](https://www.myfloridacfo.com/division/consumers/needourhelp) | Premium-issue assistance and complaint requirements |\n| [OIR contact](https://www.floir.gov/about-us/contact-us) | Regulatory routing and jurisdiction |\n\n## Value register — intentionally not prefilled\n\n- Officer payroll minimum / maximum: **TODO**. Record source, effective date, units and conversion to the exact period.\n- Other allowed remuneration exclusions: **TODO**. Record applicable rule and evidence for each deduction.\n- Experience mod(s): **TODO**. Obtain the full dated worksheet and actual exposure split at any change.\n- Expense constant and minimum premium: **TODO**. Check policy and filed rules, including their sequence and any cancellation treatment.\n- Florida assessments: **TODO**. Record the exact assessment, rate, effective date and assessable base. Do not assume one combined statewide percentage applies to every policy.\n- Carrier, re-audit and NCCI filing deadlines: **TODO**. Obtain current policy-specific requirements in writing.\n\nProject-specific assumptions belong in the case notes and never become state rules merely because a calculation passed a software test. A synthetic sample verifies code behavior, not insurance applicability.\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n",
  "gl": "# GL: the class tells you which exposure to measure\n\nGeneral liability is a separate rating system. Do not carry WC payroll exclusions, officer caps, experience mods or subcontractor decisions into GL without independent policy authority.\n\nFor each class, record its description, rating basis, rate denominator and rate from the policy schedule. The app supports payroll, gross sales, subcontract cost and units. These are input types, not a universal ISO class table. Carrier deviations, endorsements and different operations can change the answer.\n\n| Basis | Reconciliation | Common question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Payroll | Payroll register → policy-period class exposure | Which compensation items belong in this class basis? |\n| Gross sales | Sales ledger / P&L → policy-period class exposure | Which sales exclusions are actually allowed? |\n| Subcontract cost | Contracts and payment ledger → chargeable costs | Are insured subs retained, excluded or rated separately? |\n| Units | Source count → units specified by class schedule | Is the rate per unit, per thousand or another denominator? |\n\nEvery proposed excluded sale or cost should have an amount, ledger reference and rule authority. A deduction that reduces financial-statement revenue is not automatically a GL rating exclusion. Returns, taxes, freight, intercompany transactions or other categories require the applicable definition; this guide does not declare them universally excludable.\n\nFor sub-cost classes, separate insured and uninsured amounts. A COI proves information about insurance; it does not alone establish a full GL premium exclusion. When the policy rates insured subs separately, retain those costs on a separate row at the verified rate instead of subtracting them entirely. Avoid counting the same cost in more than one base.\n\nCalculate each line as chargeable exposure / stated denominator × user-supplied rate. Then follow the policy’s premium element sequence and reconcile deposits. Show unknowns as TODO. The default denominator in a new GL row is an editable input convenience, **not a statement about the applicable class**; verification is required before a final estimate appears.\n\nObtain the policy-effective ISO Commercial Lines Manual GL classification and premium-basis rules through your carrier or licensed professional. [Verisk’s PAAS catalog](https://www.verisk.com/4a5f82/siteassets/media/downloads/iso/paas/paas-professional-development-catalog.pdf) provides background on premium-audit training; it is not a current rating manual. This project does not redistribute ISO content.\n\nFor disputes, start with the carrier. NCCI’s WC process is not a GL appeal channel. See [Dispute](dispute.md).\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n",
  "wc": "# WC: payroll is the starting point, not the whole calculation\n\n## Class allocation\n\nDescribe what people actually do. Compare their work with the policy’s classifications and applicable rules. Keep supported payroll splits with time and duty records. Do not assign a low-rate class because of a job title, and do not distribute payroll among classes using an unsupported guess. The engine accepts a verified class; it does not determine the correct code.\n\n## Overtime and excluded pay\n\nGross payroll includes the ordinary pay for overtime hours. A proposed overtime deduction must identify **only the extra premium portion**, supported by payroll records and the applicable rule. Other excluded pay needs its own authority and records. Do not remove a full overtime wage line or assume all reimbursements, bonuses, vacation pay or severance are excluded.\n\n## Officers and exemptions\n\nObtain the correct minimum and maximum for the person, policy-effective rules and time period. The app accepts **already-converted period dollar limits**. It does not contain Florida values, infer weekly limits or automatically prorate annual caps. Keep the conversion in notes.\n\nUse employee-level rows for limits. If one officer works across classes or has a midterm mod / exemption change, allocate one applicable person-level limit under the governing rule before entering rows. Do not apply the full cap separately to each row. Obtain professional review for partial-period exemptions, PEOs, leased workers or other special situations.\n\nFlorida exemptions are individual and require eligibility and date checks. A certificate for a business name is not enough to assume every worker is exempt. Verify with [DWC](https://www.myfloridacfo.com/division/wc/employer/exemptions).\n\n## Subcontractors\n\nReconcile the sub ledger to contracts, work performed and dates. A 1099 does not itself establish insured status or remove exposure. A WC COI must match the entity, coverage and dates worked; verify authenticity and applicable policy/manual treatment. The engine removes a sub row only when the reviewer explicitly verifies the conditions for exclusion. Use the correct chargeable exposure, not automatically all invoices or only labor, unless the applicable rule supports that choice.\n\n## Mod and premium elements\n\nObtain the mod worksheet and effective dates. Each WC row must fall inside exactly one mod period. If a mod changes mid-policy, enter actual payroll for each segment. The engine refuses to guess a daily payroll allocation.\n\nAfter row-level mods, match the policy’s sequence for credits, discounts, minimums, expense constant and assessments. A percentage assessment step uses the running subtotal; if the assessment uses a different base, enter a separately supported fixed charge. Actual audited payroll is never prorated again. Cancellation, short-rate penalties and special rating programs require separate verification and may need manual reconciliation.\n\nCurrent values are **TODO** until verified in the applicable [NCCI Basic Manual / Atlas](https://www.ncci.com/Pages/Atlas.aspx), the policy and [Florida sources](states/fl.md). No proprietary tables or carrier rates are shipped.\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n",
  "glossary": "# Plain-English glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning in this workspace |\n| --- | --- |\n| Audit exposure | Actual payroll, sales, sub cost or units measured for the policy period |\n| Class code | A rating classification whose applicability must be checked against operations and governing rules |\n| Rate | The policy’s price per specified exposure unit; enter your own verified rate |\n| Basis / denominator | What is measured and how many exposure units one rate applies to |\n| Manual premium | Sum of class exposure × rate before the app’s subsequent rating elements |\n| Experience mod | WC experience-rating factor with an effective period; not a generic GL adjustment |\n| OT premium portion | The extra pay above straight-time for overtime work; not all overtime pay |\n| Officer limit | Applicable minimum / maximum remuneration used under verified rules for an individual and period |\n| COI | Certificate of insurance; verify issuer, entity, line and dates; not a substitute for the policy |\n| DWC exemption | Florida individual exemption subject to eligibility and effective-date requirements |\n| Minimum premium | A verified premium floor applied where the governing calculation requires |\n| Expense constant | A policy premium element whose value and treatment must be verified |\n| Assessment | A specified charge with a particular base, rate and effective rule |\n| Deposit premium | Premium credited as already paid; reconcile against the payment ledger |\n| Balance due | Final premium less credited deposit/payments, when positive |\n| Return premium | Amount of overpayment indicated by a negative final-minus-deposit balance |\n| Re-audit | Carrier review using corrected or additional records, subject to applicable requirements |\n| Standalone impact | Difference caused by one proposed adjustment within a full scenario; may overlap other impacts |\n| Verified | Reviewer checked the source, policy authority, entity, dates and applicability; not an automatic software certification |\n| TODO | Unknown or unverified; never silently substituted with a factual figure |\n\nSource register: [Florida](states/fl.md). See [WC](wc.md) and [GL](gl.md) for their different treatment.\n\n---\nCC BY-NC 4.0 · The Audit Monkey, by Sourcy. Not legal, tax or insurance advice. No agent, CPA or client relationship is created. Verify with your carrier, NCCI and a licensed professional. Verify current values.\n"
};
